From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: katamaran <katamaran@localhost>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 22:10:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] dns: resolve through the macOS system resolver

On macOS, A, CNAME, MX, NS, SRV and TXT went through net.Resolver
(res_search: a fresh query to the network every time, about 8 ms even
for a name just asked), and everything else, AAAA included, was
forwarded to the first nameserver in /etc/resolv.conf, which ignores
the scoped resolvers that VPNs and /etc/resolver add.

Ask mDNSResponder instead, for every record type, with
DNSServiceQueryRecord on one shared connection read by one goroutine.
It applies split DNS, answers from its cache in well under a
millisecond, and reports real TTLs. Names are queried as given, without
search domains. Intermediate results bring the CNAMEs on the way (the
answer is complete once records of the asked type follow) and negative
answers with their TTL; mDNSResponder does not say whether a negative
answer is NXDOMAIN or NODATA, which the resolver's address probe
settles. A failed connection fails what was pending on it and the next
query reconnects; if mDNSResponder cannot be reached at all, questions
go the old way. The cache in front empties when the DNS configuration
changes (the com.apple.system.SystemConfiguration.dns_configuration
notification), as mDNSResponder's own does.

Needs cgo; without it, or on other systems, nothing changes.
